  "description": "T1.\tHimalayan Birch\t      Rear Garden:\r\n\r\nPhytophthora bleeding canker is a plausible and common cause of lower-trunk bark damage \r\nin Himalayan birch, especially in moist or compacted soils.\r\nThe condition is often triggered or exacerbated by environmental stress, rather than occurring in isolation.\r\nVisible decay usually indicates a progressive decline process, often involving multiple agents.\r\nUnfortunately, the extent of the decay is affecting the stability of the tree and, therefore, removal is our only option.\r\n\r\nDismantle in sections using rigging equipment to a level as close as possible \r\nto the surrounding soil.\r\nGrind out the stump.\r\n\r\nThe tree's current height is 14m, and its crown spread is 6m\r\nAfter the proposed works, the tree's height is 0.0m, and the crown spread is 0.0m",
